    body: `<h2>Multi-Statutory Architecture of Maintenance</h2><p>Indian law grants maintenance remedies under multiple enactments, allowing dependent spouses, minor children, and aged parents to seek financial support according to statutory parameters.</p><h2>Key Maintenance Provisions</h2><h3>1. Section 125 Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC)</h3><p>A secular provision intended to prevent vagrancy and destitution. It empowers a Magistrate to order a person having sufficient means to pay monthly maintenance to a wife unable to maintain herself, minor children, and parents.</p><h3>2. Section 24 & 25 Hindu Marriage Act (HMA)</h3><p>Section 24 provides for <em>pendente lite</em> (interim) maintenance and litigation expenses during pending proceedings. Section 25 empowers the court to grant permanent alimony at the time of passing a decree.</p><h2>Social Justice Interpretation</h2><p>As affirmed in <em>Badshah v. Sou. Urmila Badshah Godse</em>, maintenance provisions are social justice legislation interpreted purposively to protect dependent spouses and prevent hardship.</p>`
